      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To evaluate \\(i^{11} + i^{111}\\), we first need to understand the powers of the imaginary unit \\(i\\). The powers of \\(i\\) cycle every four terms as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ni^1 &= i, \\\\\ni^2 &= -1, \\\\\ni^3 &= -i, \\\\\ni^4 &= 1, \\\\\ni^5 &= i, \\text{ and so on.}\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThis pattern repeats every four powers. Therefore, to find \\(i^{11}\\) and \\(i^{111}\\), we need to determine the remainder when 11 and 111 are divided by 4.\n\nFirst, we calculate the remainder of 11 divided by 4:\n\\[\n11 \\div 4 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 3.\n\\]\nSo, \\(11 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\). This means \\(i^{11} = i^3\\). From the cycle, we know that \\(i^3 = -i\\).\n\nNext, we calculate the remainder of 111 divided by 4:\n\\[\n111 \\div 4 = 27 \\text{ remainder } 3.\n\\]\nSo, \\(111 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{4}\\). This means \\(i^{111} = i^3\\). Again, from the cycle, we know that \\(i^3 = -i\\).\n\nNow, we can add the two results together:\n\\[\ni^{11} + i^{111} = i^3 + i^3 = -i + (-i) = -2i.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{-2i}.\n\\]",
